  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "A robot starts at the bottom of a staircase with $n$ steps, where $n$ is an integer chosen uniformly at random from $1$ to $10.$ The robot then climbs a uniformly random number of steps between $1$ and $n.$\n\nWhat is the expected number of steps the robot climbs?\n\nThe expected number of steps the robot climbs is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "This is a two-step random process: first, an integer $n$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ through $10,$ and then a number is chosen uniformly from $1$ through $n.$\n\n- Let $Y$ be the first random number: the number of steps in the staircase. Since an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $10,$ we have $Y∼U\\{1,2,…,10\\}.$\n\n- Let $X$ be the number of steps the robot climbs, an integer selected uniformly from $1$ to $Y.$\n\nWe are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Since the distribution of $X$ depends on $Y,$ we apply the tower property:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]]\n$$\n\nGiven $Y=n,$ the number $X$ is chosen uniformly from $1$ to $n.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X∣Y=n]=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\frac{Y+1}{2}].\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{E}[Y+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Y$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,…,10\\},$ we use the formula for the mean of the first $n$ integers:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{10+1}{2}=5.5\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we finally get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\text{E}[Y]+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (5.5+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6.5 \\\\  & =3.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
